.TH "OSTRYA\-SIGN" "1" "" "ostrya" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ostrya\-sign \- Add or verify signatures on a commit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ostrya sign [OPTIONS] [COMMIT] [KEY_ID]...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Add or verify signatures on a commit.
.PP
The signatures are in the detached metadata of the commit. Without \%\-\-verify, the command signs the commit once for each key and prints nothing. Each key takes the engine of the \%\-\-sign\-type before it. With \%\-\-verify, it verifies with one verifier for each engine, prints one line for each signature and a verdict for each engine, and exits \%1 if an engine has no valid signature. A verify with no key and no \%\-\-remote reads the key directories \fB\%/etc/ostree\fR, \fB\%/usr/share/ostree\fR, and each \%\-\-keys\-dir, and the GPG system trust. The command has no option that removes a signature.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fICOMMIT\fR
The commit to operate on (a checksum or a ref). Required.
.TP
\fIKEY_ID...\fR
Key identifiers: \%base64 keys, or gpg key selectors.
.IP
The \%\-\-sign\-type before a \%KEY\-ID selects its engine. For \%ed25519 and spki, each \%KEY\-ID is a \%base64 key: a secret key to sign, or a public key to verify. An spki secret key is a \%PKCS#8 DER, a \%SEC1 DER that names the \%P\-256 or the \%P\-384 curve, or a \%PKCS#1 DER of an RSA key. An spki public key is a SubjectPublicKeyInfo DER. The \%base64 of an spki key can hold line breaks. For gpg, each \%KEY\-ID is a signing key that gpg resolves (a fingerprint, key id, or user id). A verify refuses a gpg \%KEY\-ID: a gpg verify reads \%\-\-keys\-file, \%\-\-remote, or the system trust.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-verify\fR
Verify the signatures of the commit, and add no signature.
.IP
The command verifies with one verifier for each engine of the run. For each engine, it prints one line for each signature, with \fB\%good\fR, \fB\%BAD\fR, or \fB\%no public key\fR\&. Then it prints \fB\%verification OK\fR or \fB\%verification FAILED\fR\&. If the commit has no signature for the engine, the command prints \fB\%no signatures found\fR\&. With more than one engine in the report, a line with the engine name and a colon comes before the lines of each engine. If an engine in the report has no valid signature, the command exits \%1.
.IP
With a \%KEY\-ID or a \%\-\-keys\-file, the engines are the engines of these keys. With \%\-\-remote, the engines are the engines that the config of the remote turns on. With no key and no \%\-\-remote, the engines are the engines that have a key in the key directories or in the GPG system trust. If one of these engines finds a signature, the command skips each engine that finds none, and prints nothing for it. A \%\-\-sign\-type with no key selects its engine, and the command verifies with the selected engines alone. Each selected engine must then have a valid signature. The command refuses a run with no engine. If the gpg engine is not excluded by a \%\-\-sign\-type, a keyring of the GPG system trust that cannot be read refuses the run.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-sign\-type\fR \fINAME\fR
The signature engine of the \%KEY\-ID and \%\-\-keys\-file keys after it.
.IP
The names are \fB\%ed25519\fR, \fB\%spki\fR, and \fB\%gpg\fR\&. The engine selects the key format and the \%detached\-metadata key that holds the signatures: \fB\%ostree.sign.ed25519\fR, \fB\%ostree.sign.spki\fR, or \fB\%ostree.gpgsigs\fR\&. Each key takes the engine of the nearest \%\-\-sign\-type before it on the command line. A key before the first \%\-\-sign\-type takes \fB\%ed25519\fR\&. The option can occur more than once, and each occurrence needs a key of its own: a key after it and before the next \%\-\-sign\-type. A \%\-\-verify run with no key on the command line takes each \%\-\-sign\-type as a selection of its engine, and each selected engine must have a valid signature.
.TP
\fB\-\-keys\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR
Read keys from a file.
.IP
The \%\-\-sign\-type before the file selects its engine. For \%ed25519, the file has \%base64 keys, one per line: secret keys to sign, public keys to verify. For spki, the file holds PEM \fB\%PRIVATE KEY\fR blocks, each a \%PKCS#8 DER of a \%P\-256, a \%P\-384, an \%Ed25519, or an RSA key, to sign, and PEM \fB\%PUBLIC KEY\fR blocks to verify. Each \fB\%PRIVATE KEY\fR block adds one signature. Signing reads every file. A signing file is a regular file of at most \%1 MiB. An \%ed25519 or spki verify reads the last file of its engine. With this option or a \%KEY\-ID, a verify does not read \%\-\-keys\-dir or the key directories.
.IP
For gpg, the file is a binary or armored keyring for verify, and every file is read. A gpg verify refuses a path that names no regular file. A gpg signing run refuses this option.
.TP
\fB\-\-keys\-dir\fR \fIPATH\fR
Add a directory to the key directories of a verify.
.IP
The key directories are \fB\%/etc/ostree\fR, \fB\%/usr/share/ostree\fR, and each \%\-\-keys\-dir, in this order. A verify with no key and no \%\-\-remote reads \fB\%trusted.<type>\fR, \fB\%revoked.<type>\fR, and the files in \fB\%trusted.<type>.d/\fR and \fB\%revoked.<type>.d/\fR of each directory, for the \%ed25519 and spki engines. Without a \%\-\-sign\-type, if one engine finds a signature, the verify skips each engine that finds none. A revoked key of one directory applies to the keys of each directory. An \%ed25519 file has one \%base64 key per line, and an spki file has PEM \fB\%PUBLIC KEY\fR blocks. The option can occur more than once. An empty value names the working directory. The command refuses this option with \%\-\-remote.
.TP
\fB\-\-gpg\-homedir\fR \fIGPG_HOMEDIR\fR
The GnuPG home directory where gpg resolves signing keys.
.IP
Without this option, gpg uses its own resolution. The command refuses it if no key and no \%\-\-sign\-type names the gpg engine.
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR \fIREMOTE\fR
Verify with the keys of this remote.
.IP
The engines are the engines that the config of the remote turns on for its commits: \fB\%gpg\-verify\fR and \fB\%sign\-verify\fR, or the trust group that the key \fB\%trust\fR names. The keys are the keys of the remote alone: \fB\%<remote>.trustedkeys.gpg\fR in the repository and in \fB\%/etc/ostree/remotes.d/\fR, each \fB\%gpgkeypath\fR entry, and the \fB\%verification\-<type>\-key\fR and \fB\%verification\-<type>\-file\fR keys, or the keys of the trust group. The command reads no global GPG trusted directory and no key directory. A \%\-\-sign\-type selects its engine. The command then reads the keys of the selected engines alone, and refuses an engine that the config does not turn on. The command refuses this option with a \%KEY\-ID, a \%\-\-keys\-file, or a \%\-\-keys\-dir, and without \%\-\-verify.
.PP
The global options are listed in \fBostrya\fR(1).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBostrya\fR(1)
